<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<topic id="1a01e248-9fbf-4065-84c2-89fce456723c" revisionNumber="1">
  <developerConceptualDocument xmlns="http://ddue.schemas.microsoft.com/authoring/2003/5" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">
    <introduction>
      <para>
      TheIntensive.Services.CTKAPIWrapper Library is a set of .NET classes that make it easier for .NET applications
      to communicate with CORE using the CTKAPI web endpoints.  
      The classes are grouped into three namespaces.
      </para>
		<table>
		  <tableHeader>
		    <row>
		      <entry><para>Namespace</para></entry>
		      <entry><para>Description</para></entry>
		    </row>
		  </tableHeader>
		  <row>
		      <entry><para><codeEntityReference>N:Rackspace.CTKAPI.Net</codeEntityReference></para></entry>
		      <entry>
		      	<para>
		      		These classes provide "low-level" functionality to build a request or an action, 
		      		submit it to the CORE CTKAPI endpoint and perform some basic formatting of the returned data.
		      	</para>
		      </entry>
		  </row>
		  <row>
		      <entry><para><codeEntityReference>N:Rackspace.CTKAPI.Net.CTKObjects</codeEntityReference></para></entry>
		      <entry>
		      	<para>
		      		These are a set of "convenience" classes that make working with common objects a little easier. They are designed to mimic
		      		the CORE CTKAPI objects as much as possible.  
		        </para>
			  </entry>
		  </row>
		  <row>
		      <entry><para><codeEntityReference>N:Rackspace.CTKAPI.Net.Exceptions</codeEntityReference></para></entry>
		      <entry ><para>Contains all of the exceptions that can be thrown by any class in the Library</para></entry>
		  </row>
		</table>
		<para>
			This documentation assumes that you are familiar with the 
        	<externalLink>
			    <linkText>CORE CTKAPI REST API</linkText>
			    <linkUri>https://ws.core.rackspace.com/ctkapi</linkUri>
			</externalLink>
		</para>
		<!--<mediaLink> <image placement="center" xlink:href="SDLC_Future"/><>/mediaLink> -->

    </introduction>
    <!-- Add one or more top-level section elements.  These are collapsible.
         If using <autoOutline />, add an address attribute to identify it
         and specify a title so that it can be jumped to with a hyperlink. -->
    <section address="Getting Started">
      <title>Getting Started</title>
      <content>
        <!-- Uncomment this to create a sub-section outline
        <autoOutline /> -->
        <para>
        	Before using theIntensive.Services.CTKAPIWrapper Library, you will need to update your project references
        	to include a reference to <literal>Rackspace.CTKAPI.Net.dll</literal> file. 
        </para>
        
        <para>
        	Then your code
        	will need to establish a connection to a CORE environment by instantiating the
        	<codeEntityReference>T:Rackspace.CTKAPI.Net.CTKAPI</codeEntityReference> class.	By default, 
        	this will establish a connection to the CORE PRODUCTION environment, however, you can 
        	tell the constructor to connect to the DEV or STAGING CORE environment as well.  This 
        	instance variable will be used to submit Queries and Actions, or when instantiating one 
        	of the <codeEntityReference>N:Rackspace.CTKAPI.Net.CTKObjects</codeEntityReference> classes.
        </para>
        
        <para>
        	You can then build Queries and Actions using the low-level classes in the 
     		<codeEntityReference>N:Rackspace.CTKAPI.Net</codeEntityReference> namespace or
        	you can instatiate one or more <codeEntityReference>N:Rackspace.CTKAPI.Net.CTKObjects</codeEntityReference>
        	classes.  Either way
        	you will need to understand how to build and combine
        	<codeEntityReference>T:Rackspace.CTKAPI.Net.CTKWhereCondition</codeEntityReference>
        	objects and <codeEntityReference>T:Rackspace.CTKAPI.Net.CTKWhere</codeEntityReference>
        	objects.  
        </para>
        <para>
			Finally, as a best practice, you should always call the 
			<codeEntityReference>M:Rackspace.CTKAPI.Net.CTKAPI.Logout</codeEntityReference> method to log out
			of CORE and invalidate the current token.
        </para>
        
        <para>
        	Refer to the <codeEntityReference>T:Rackspace.CTKAPI.Net.CTKQuery</codeEntityReference>, 
        	<codeEntityReference>T:Rackspace.CTKAPI.Net.CTKAction</codeEntityReference>, and 
        	<codeEntityReference>T:Rackspace.CTKAPI.Net.CTKObjects.CTKAccount</codeEntityReference>
        	for full coding examples.
        </para>
      </content>
      <!-- If a section contains a sections element, its content creates
           sub-sections.  These are not collapsible.
      <sections>
